<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Lattice SpotBugs exclusions. Kept deliberately small: only well-understood false positives that
  SpotBugs cannot model, never a way to silence a real finding.

  EI_EXPOSE_REP2 in the service and routes layers: a service stores its injected repository, and a
  route handler stores its injected service. These are shared, stateless collaborators wired once by
  dependency injection (the standard pattern), not mutable state to defend by copying. Defensively
  copying a repository or a service is nonsensical, so the "stored a reference to a mutable object"
  heuristic is a false positive here. It is also a borderline (priority 2) finding that flips
  non-deterministically under memory pressure in the full reactor build, so it is excluded for the
  two layers where all such constructor injection lives, across every service module.

  The two below were surfaced by scanning test sources, which was previously switched off. Both are
  scoped to integration-test classes by name (the *IT convention), so neither can ever hide the same
  finding in shipped code - which is the point: a hardcoded credential or a plaintext socket in a
  service is exactly what this gate is for.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
    <Or>
      <Package name="~io\.lattice\..*\.service"/>
      <Package name="~io\.lattice\..*\.routes"/>
    </Or>
  </Match>

  <!--
    HARD_CODE_PASSWORD in integration tests: the credential is the Testcontainers broker's own,
    declared a few lines away in the same test that starts the container. There is no secret here to
    leak - the value exists only for a container that lives and dies inside the test run, and reading
    it from the environment would mean the suite could not start a broker without external setup.

    REMOVE THIS when a test authenticates against something it did not itself create, because at that
    point the credential is real and belongs in configuration rather than in a source file.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="HARD_CODE_PASSWORD"/>
    <Class name="~io\.lattice\..*IT"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    UNENCRYPTED_SERVER_SOCKET in integration tests: every occurrence is `new ServerSocket(0)` used to
    reserve a free ephemeral port, closed immediately, with nothing ever sent over it. There is no
    traffic to encrypt, so TLS would protect an empty channel. The same pattern already carries a
    documented `nosemgrep` marker for Semgrep's equivalent rule, so this records the same judgement
    for the other engine rather than making a new one.

    REMOVE THIS if a test ever serves real traffic over a socket it opens itself, which would be a
    genuine finding rather than a port reservation.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="UNENCRYPTED_SERVER_SOCKET"/>
    <Class name="~io\.lattice\..*IT"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    COMMAND_INJECTION in the broker-certificate suite: it runs the JDK's own keytool to generate a
    throwaway keystore for a TLS listener the test starts. Every argument is a compile-time literal
    and the executable is resolved from java.home, so no part of the command line comes from input.
    keytool was chosen over a certificate-generation library precisely to avoid adding a dependency
    for a fixture, and it is what issue-certs.sh already uses.

    REMOVE THIS if any argument to that process ever becomes a variable, because a constructed
    command line is the finding this rule exists to catch.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="COMMAND_INJECTION"/>
    <Class name="io.lattice.common.mesh.BrokerCertificateIT"/>
  </Match>
</FindBugsFilter>
